
Repository for:

An Integrated Population Model to Incorporate Spatio-Temporal Heterogeneity in Demographic Rates

Folder description:

- packages_needed.R: Script to install and load all required R packages used in the simulation study and case study analyses.

- Case study: Contains scripts to reproduce the spatially explicit integrated population model (sIPM) analysis of Gray Catbird data, including model fitting, posterior summaries, spatial predictions, and figure generation. This folder also includes the cleaned data required to reproduce the analysis.

- Simulation study: Contains scripts to generate simulated datasets, fit the proposed sIPM, evaluate model performance, and reproduce all simulation results presented in the manuscript.

Software:

- The nearest-neighbour Gaussian process (NNGP) used in the manuscript was first implemented as standalone R functions (Case Study/NNGP_functions.R), and the manuscript results were produced with them. These functions have since been developed, documented and tested as part of the R package BayesNSGP (https://github.com/danielturek/BayesNSGP): neighbor construction, NNGP density, simulation, a local-update MCMC sampler (RW_NN_GP) and posterior prediction (NNGP.pred). The package is described in Ketwaroo and Turek (submitted to the Journal of Open Source Software; [add DOI / arXiv link when available]).

- The Case Study folder contains, in addition to the original scripts, a re-run of the case study with BayesNSGP and a comparison of run time and posterior estimates with the original implementation (see Case Study/README, section "Re-running the case study with BayesNSGP"). The original scripts and results are unchanged.

Installation:

- Run packages_needed.R to install the required CRAN and GitHub packages. The BayesNSGP scripts additionally need the development version of BayesNSGP (the release on CRAN does not yet include all the changes they use):

    remotes::install_github("danielturek/BayesNSGP", subdir = "BayesNSGP")

- All scripts assume that the working directory is the root of this repository (the folder containing "Case Study" and "Simulation study").
